WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.470
PETE: Okay, so you're in the Rails shop, you're
all in the cloud, you're using a lot of different

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:11.250
Amazon services and you mentioned that you
had a lot of different sort of data components

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.559
or data services that you're using.

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:18.850
Tell us, for those of our audience who don't
know, tell us a little bit about the difference

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:22.250
between the AWS Data options.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.950
VIDA: Right.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:30.630
So we use a lot of our data we use to store
regular SQL type of storage because it's quick

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:36.260
to access, we can query across it, things
like that so, you know, we have a concept

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.910
of a retailer with their campaigns and the
offers that they are trying to give out to

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:40.910
their customers.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:46.249
We use MySQL or the SQL Data store, to store
all that kind of information.

00:00:46.249 --> 00:00:49.460
But then we also have a really large number
of user objects.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.949
So not only do we have to deal with scaling
- we'll only have a certain number of clients

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:56.309
that are giving our offers, fairly small.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:00.460
But the number of consumers that are redeeming
these offers are magnitudes larger.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.859
So we have a lot more objects in that space.

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:10.090
And it needs to be global - global data store
so we want to have just one database of users

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:15.050
across the whole world rather than partitioning
out, partition out some of our enterprise

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.350
clients on the different servers and things
like that.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:24.850
So for our user store, we're actually using
Simple DB to store some of the fast user data.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:31.820
And then we're also using offline storage
because part of our process as a user, Facebook

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.500
Connects with us, and gives us their data
such as Likes that we can use to predict what

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.940
are the things, offers they'd be interested
in from us and we're also just trying to understand

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.090
the shopping habits of these users according
to their social data.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.260
So that data is actually quite large.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.670
We could use a blob store in SQL to store
it but it makes more sense to just, we don't

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.640
need it to be served up.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.550
It's just purely for offline processing so
we actually write it out to S3 to disc.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.550
PETE: Okay.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.260
Can you give me a quick use case on both sides
of that fence?

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.179
Like, what's an example of the type of data
you are using real time and what's an example

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:10.739
of the type of data that you're interested
in more batch mode?

00:02:10.739 --> 00:02:14.489
VIDA: In real time we maybe just want to know
how many friends you have and how many likes

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:18.879
you have in aggregate, so I just stores an
integer to say, how many friends and how many

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:20.020
likes you have.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.769
In offline mode, a lot of our retailers are
interested in what are the top likes of our

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:25.849
users.

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:31.959
So, we have to actually go in and store individual
users and even to the granularity of what's

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:38.489
the most popular in the retail category versus
the music category.

